  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do traffic lights know a car is waiting?",
      "a": "Many junctions have wire loops buried under the road. A car's metal body changes the loop's magnetic field, which the controller detects. Others use cameras or radar.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the lines cut into the road at traffic lights?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does pressing the crossing button do anything?",
      "a": "At most junctions, yes: it asks the controller for a pedestrian phase. The green man comes when it can safely fit into the cycle, so it may take a while.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the green man take so long?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a traffic light controller do?",
      "a": "It is a computer that decides which phase comes next and how long each lasts, using sensor readings and its timing plan. It is built so it can never show conflicting greens.",
      "asks": [
        "Who controls traffic lights?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a phase at traffic lights?",
      "a": "A phase is a group of movements that can safely go together, like all north-south traffic. The controller cycles through the phases in turn.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do traffic lights take turns?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a green wave?",
      "a": "A green wave is when lights along a road are timed so that a car driving at the speed limit meets green after green. Traffic centres coordinate the timings.",
      "asks": [
        "How are traffic lights linked together?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Inductive loops buried in roads detect vehicles by the change their metal makes to the loop's magnetic field.",
    "Traffic light controllers are designed so that conflicting directions can never show green together.",
    "Adaptive systems such as SCOOT, used in the UK, keep adjusting signal timings to match the traffic."
  ],
